so tory leadership candidates rishi  sunak and liz trust are continuing to  campaign today following last night's  hustings in eastbourne  let's get the views now of the  conservative mp and liz trust supporter  jake berry who joins us now jay barry  thanks very much indeed for being here  with us this morning good morning i just  wanted to to reflect on some of the  things that liz trust said last night  but also in an interview with the  financial times this morning the country  as everyone will know now  is facing a big hike in energy bills is  going on already they're set to spiral  even further  liz trust has said that tax cuts not  handouts are the way to deal with that  so can you explain that to me is she  ruling out of an expansion to the  support schemes that we've seen so far  400 pounds coming from that at this  autumn for people is she ruling out an  expansion of those schemes  well you're absolutely right to say  there's significant support already  available and in fact it's 1200 pounds  for  houses on lower income but this has been  absolutely clear that the next step the  government should take  is knock off those levies some of the  what people call the green taxes which  would be another  153 pound saving i think that's a  significant cost of living saving  for families up and down this country um  who knows what happens next year next  year is another year i think we've  learned from the bank of england this  week that energy prices won't be going  down in the in the short term so i guess  that nothing is off the table but liz  has been absolutely clear that the next  step she will take and is committed to  is knock off  well over 100 pounds off your bill  through removal of some of the levees  but that but that's a drop in the ocean  we're talking about uh bills in excess  of 3 000 pounds i mean the most  vulnerable the people that are really  struggling they just won't be able to  afford to pay doesn't she need to do  more isn't  isn't handouts a necessity in this  situation  well you're absolutely right to say that  it does feel like a small contribution  all of it to what is the cost of living  crisis but you know the most needed  households will get in excess of 1200  pounds and that will be on top of  support they've already received but  actually that's why liz trusts you know  the way she's going to unify our party  and bring our country back together is  by helping people by leaving more money  in their pockets through  tax cuts um we're the only country in  the world almost in the developed world  that's responding to a cost of living  crisis by deciding what we should do is  take more money away from people by  increasing their taxes that is clearly  the wrong economic priority and that's  why liz is committed to cut taxes not  you know for ordinary working families  in this country who will be struggling  with that cost of living and that's why  it's the right thing to do  so can you explain to me how tax cuts  will help people with  the immediate problem of paying an  energy bill for example and  the most vulnerable people who who are  on benefits those who own below the  allowances won't get any benefit from  the cancellation of the rise in national  insurance  for example so so how are they going to  be helped by tax cuts  well look um  you know i've already said i've been  really clear that there's 1200 pounds  already available for those families and  on top of that liz has committed for  over 150 pounds additional support by  removing the levees  it's not just people on low income in  this country who are struggling with  this cost of living crisis i absolutely  appreciate they've got the highest  mountain to climb but it's mid middle  income families with mums and dads out  working who are paying the highest tax  burden for 70 years and it's about  giving everyone who's paying tax a break  by putting more money in their pocket  i mean rishi sunak has been saying that  the tories will lose the next election  if inflation isn't kept under control he  says that these tax cut proposals from  liz trust are inflationary that's a risk  isn't it  well of course there's always a risk i'm  not sure it's an orthodoxy i accept it's  obviously currently a treasury orthodoxy  but i don't think this cost of living  crisis is being caused by people with  too much money in their pocket going  around and spending money it's being  caused by a huge increase in things like  your diesel price or your petrol price  or your gas price it's a sort of a it's  an energy push uh inflation spike rather  than the consumer spending inflation  spike  and that's why liz is right to say let's  enable people to keep more of their  money in the first place and just in  terms of who's best place to win the  election list trust absolutely is the  only person in my view who's best place  to beat kier stahlmann because she will  unite our party  and bring us back together in common  cause to take on the real risk of this  country and its economy which is the  labour party  some people watching might argue though  they might question  why are you committing to spending  billions cancelling that hike in  national insurance which will largely  benefit the better off when that money  could be channeled and targeted towards  the most vulnerable people  well you're absolutely wrong actually  increases in national insurance hurt the  people on lowest wages the most  lower your wage the bigger proportion of  your wage you spend on national  insurance so canceling it is absolutely  the right thing to do to help people on  low wages  and what about  the issue of leveling up i know it's  something that liz trust is going to be  focusing on today something you're very  interested in yourself in fact  i know that you've been very critical on  twitter of rishi sunak after a video  emerged yesterday of him in tunbridge  wells talking about diverting funds from  deprived urban areas to areas like that  the point he says he was making was that  he wants to help  make sure that help goes to deprived  areas outside of big cities do you  accept that that's what his intention  was with those comments  well i'm i'm here at home in the east  lancashire and probably what is a  deprived urban area people look at the  chancellor's comments um of the acts  chancellor's comments and make that i  don't mind about rishi's priorities but  what i would say  is i served in boris johnson's first  cabinet as the northern powerhouse  minister that sort of view  is the overriding treasury orthodoxy and  one of the reasons i'm supporting liz  trust is she is a woman of action who  will take on the status quo who will  ensure that we break this sort of  country where the cards are always  stacked in favor of the wealthy areas in  the south of england she will break that  down and she's committed to this  leveling up formula  which will see fairer funding across our  united kingdom and if what  rishi has said wasn't the overriding  view of the treasury  you wouldn't need a leveling up formula  because you would already be spending  money equally across our united kingdom  we are not but that's why liz's  commitment to create a leveling up  formula with fair funding across the uk  spreading opportunity wherever you live  is absolutely the right thing to do and  shows that she's best placed to win a  general election and unite our party but  but hasn't the north recently benefited  from the leveling up formula i mean you  obviously represent and  you're in the northern research group as  well you head it um the north has  benefited from the leveling up agenda  that's been pushed through to date  hasn't it well it has to some extent  first of all the leveling up formula is  something that the northern research  group more specifically me and a few  colleagues have been designing and  working on and that's why we're so  pleased that liz has committed to take  it  and implement it in government but just  to put in context why do we think we  need it over the last decade 10 years of  conservative government i admit  if we'd have spent the same on transport  infrastructure in the north as we have  in london and the south east we would  have spent an additional 66 billion  pounds in fact transport spending in  london is 15 higher than the national  average that cannot be right and that's  why we're so pleased that liz  as our new prime minister  if she gets there is committed to  equalizing fairer funding across the uk  i know what i would expect that  additional six and a half billion a year  on in the north of england i would have  built northern powerhouse rail i'm very  pleased to see that this trust is  committed to that as well which to me  shows she's the real person for  levelling up  okay jake berry we appreciate your time  today thanks very much indeed
 

